## Understanding California Lemon Law

Step one in a Los Angeles lemon law claim is to understand the overarching concepts of CA lemon law.

California lemon law - also referred to as the [Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act](https://library.nclc.org/book/consumer-warranty-law/126-california-song-beverly-consumer-warranty-act) - was enacted to hold manufacturers accountable for selling defective vehicles.

Now, simply noticing your vehicle has a defect does not automatically qualify it for a claim under [California’s lemon law](/info/a-comprehensive-guide-to-california-lemon-law). The manufacturer must be given a reasonable number of repair attempts to fix the defect. If they are unsuccessful, you may be eligible to seek benefits.

For your vehicle to qualify for a claim under the lemon law in Los Angeles, the following criteria must be met:

- The vehicle was covered under the original manufacturer warranty or the dealer warranty when the defect was first reported to the manufacturer.

- The defect substantially impairs the vehicle’s safety, functionality, and/or value.

- The defect was not caused by driver abuse, error, or neglect.

- The manufacturer’s certified repair facility has made a reasonable number of attempts to fix the defects - which were unsuccessful; or

- The vehicle was in the manufacturer-certified repair facility for 30 or more cumulative days.

If you have a paper trail to prove your [vehicle meets these qualifications](/blog/what-records-are-needed-in-california-lemon-law-cases) - repair orders, communication with the manufacturer, warranty terms, etc. - you may take the next step in seeking compensation via the state’s lemon law.

